 All experiments below use input injection and, where tables are enabled, a table learning rate $128$ times the backbone learning rate. The frequency analysis uses seeds 42, 43 and 44; the remaining experiments use seed 42. All use the online gap of Section~\ref{sec:setting}.
 
 \paragraph{Context frequency.}
-A finite corpus does not fully represent the continuation distribution of each context: a context whose population distribution assigns equal probability to $C$ and $D$ may appear three times with $C$ and once with $D$ in training, so fitting the sample underpredicts $D$ on fresh data. Enabling both tables ($2^{20}$ rows each) and measuring online gaps at step 1000, grouped by the context count $F$ in the unrepeated corpus, shows that low-frequency contexts have systematically larger gaps (Figure~\ref{fig:frequency}; markers are native-bin means, dashed lines the coverage-based fits of Section~\ref{sec:coverage}).
