 \paragraph{Table capacity.}
 Bigger tables make forking worse. Across three decades of table rows $R$ in bigram-only and trigram-only models, the step-1000 gap generally increases, sublinearly on log-log axes, with the bigram curve flattening toward the upper end of the measured range (Figure~\ref{fig:capacity}).
 
 \paragraph{Training corpus size.}
-Bigger corpora make forking milder. In a trigram-only model with $2^{20}$ rows trained for three epochs, varying the corpus over 20 sizes from 6M to 994M prediction tokens per epoch ($0.125D_1$--$20D_1$, where $D_1$ is the single-shard budget of 337 batches), the epoch-2 and epoch-3 end gaps fall by more than an order of magnitude as the corpus grows, reaching 0.13 and 0.22 nats at $20D_1$ (Figure~\ref{fig:corpus-size}). Two factors can contribute to the decay: a context's unseen-continuation mass decreases with its training count (Section~\ref{sec:coverage}), and, at fixed table size, a larger corpus spreads each table row over more distinct contexts, diluting per-context memorization. Both factors point the same way for production pretraining---a single pass over a corpus far larger than the memory---where the coverage account predicts weaker forking; the adverse corner, small corpora replayed for many passes, is exactly post-training, which we test in Section~\ref{sec:transfer}.
