 \section{Mathematical Derivations and Model Tests}
 \label{app:math}
 
+\subsection{From novel continuations to the frequency law}
+\label{app:coverage}
+
+Fix a training step $t$ and group prediction positions by the training count $F\ge1$ of their context. Within a frequency bin, let $f(F)$ be the fraction of validation positions whose true continuation never followed that context in training. The token itself may be common; what training never showed is the context--continuation pair. The gap splits exactly into two terms:
+\begin{equation}
+\begin{aligned}
+\gap_t(F)&=f(F)D_t(F)+\big(1-f(F)\big)S_t(F),\\
+D_t(F)&=\ell^{\mathrm{val}}_{\mathrm{novel},t}(F)-\ell^{\mathrm{tr}}_t(F),\qquad
+S_t(F)=\ell^{\mathrm{val}}_{\mathrm{seen},t}(F)-\ell^{\mathrm{tr}}_t(F).
+\end{aligned}
+\label{eq:gap-decomp}
+\end{equation}
+Here $D_t$ and $S_t$ compare novel and seen validation losses with the same online training loss. All three losses are bin means at the same logged step, rather than losses matched context by context. Coverage sets the mixture weights; the model determines the two loss differences.
+
+If both differences vary little with $F$, this decomposition is approximately affine in the novel fraction:
+\begin{equation}
+\gap_t(F)\approx a_t f(F)+b_t.
+\label{eq:gap_j}
+\end{equation}
+When the seen term is also negligible, it reduces to $\gap_t(F)\approx D_t f(F)$: the gap is the fraction exposed to crowding-out times its loss penalty. The affine relation describes the measured bins well (Figure~\ref{fig:coverage}b,c), but the fitted intercept is not generally the seen term; frequency-dependent components can also produce a close fit.
+
+Good--Turing connects missing continuation mass to singleton counts (Appendix~\ref{app:gt-identity}). For a fixed context with a conditional tail $p_{(r)}\sim A r^{-s}$, the expected missing mass decays as $F^{-(1-1/s)}$ over an intermediate range (Equation~\eqref{eq:occupancy}). This gives a source of slow, approximately power-law decay. Pooling heterogeneous contexts and including the seen term can change the gap's slope, so coverage helps explain the shape without fixing a universal exponent. Appendix~\ref{app:coverage-tests} tests the approximation and its limits.
+
